<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Angola</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Angola is a country in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> that was a Portuguese colony from the late sixteenth century until 1975. It was a major source of enslaved Africans for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, especially to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Portuguese expansion from Angola affected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire</a>'s trade, and the colonial war that began in 1961 contributed to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angola.jpg" alt="Angola" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Henrique de Carvalho Santos Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Angola.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Long-distance trade linked Bulozi westwards to <b>Angola</b> through the Mambari, Ovimbundu merchants who exchanged cloth and guns for ivory and slaves, and from the 1870s southwards through traders such as George Westbeech.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Discovered in 1789 before the rising, it ended with eleven exiled to <b>Angola</b> and Joaquim José da Silva Xavier, Tiradentes, hanged in Rio de Janeiro in 1792; he became the symbol of Brazilian independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1683 they operated in eastern <b>Angola</b>, and Portuguese efforts to stop them failed; the Vili also took up smithing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Ndongo took shape among the Mbundu, Kimbundu-speaking farmers of the highlands between the Lukala and Kwanza rivers in what is now northern <b>Angola</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ndongo">Kingdom of Ndongo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Portuguese Empire that grew from these voyages lasted until 1999, when Macau was handed to China, and left Portuguese as the language of Brazil, <b>Angola</b>, Mozambique and other countries, today spoken by more than 250 million people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The turning point came in 1665 at the Battle of Mbwila, where Kongo forces met Portuguese troops from <b>Angola</b> and their African allies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kongo-empire">Kongo Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
